72:This study was approved by the research ethics committee of the First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University (2018-KY-88). A total of 496 patients with psychiatric disorders and 255 heathy controls from three independent datasets (one discovery dataset and two validation datasets) were included in this study. Additionally, another extensive dataset comprising 492 healthy individuals aged 19 -80 was included to construct a normal structural covariance network (see Methods). The discovery dataset was obtained from the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA) Consortium for Neuropsychiatric Phenomics (CNP) and is available on the public database OpenfMRI (Poldrack et al., 2016).
84:First,  we  estimated  individualized  gray  matter  morphological  abnormalities using the normative modeling. Following methods used in previous  studies  (Han  et  al.,  2023a;  Marquand  et  al.,  2016;  Wolfers et al., 2018), we trained a Gaussian process regression model to estimate the normative range of regional gray matter volumes (GMVs) based on age and sex in HCs. This trained model was then applied to each patient, generating predicted GMVs. Subsequently, Z scores (representing individualized abnormalities) were calculated to quantify deviations of the predicted GMVs from normal distributions. Positive Z scores indicated higher GMVs in patients compared to HCs, and vice versa. This process resulted in a Z -score vector (246 × 1) for each patient, representing their individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities.
96:In our study, we aimed to investigate whether structural abnormalities of the identified subtypes were also influenced by the normal brain network, as assessed through the structural covariance network. This network delineates the coordination of regional volumes among various brain  regions,  potentially  reflecting  shared  developmental  pathways (Alexander-Bloch et al., 2013a; Han et al., 2023c; Yun et al., 2015) and its dysfunction was implicated in the pathology of depression (Kaiser et al., 2015; Lima-Ojeda et al., 2018; Sotiras et al., 2017; Yun et al., 2020). Importantly, previous research has indicated that the structural covariance  network  exhibits  a  stronger  association  with  gray  matter morphological abnormalities in depression compared to the functional network (Han et al., 2023b).
98:To construct the normal structural covariance network, we utilized another  extensive  dataset  from  a  single  site  (SALD),  comprising  492 healthy individuals aged 19 -80 years. Further details about this dataset are  available  elsewhere  (Wei  et  al.,  2018).  The  normal  structural covariance network was established by computing pairwise correlation coefficients of regional gray matter volumes across subjects, resulting in a 246 × 246 structural covariance (SC) network where negative correlations were adjusted to zero (Dong et al., 2024; Li et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2023). We assessed the relationship between the SC network and the gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identified subtypes, employing methodologies established in prior studies (Li et al., 2023; Shafiei et al., 2020a). For each subtype, the normalized collective abnormalities/differences  of  structural  neighbors  of  region  i  ( D sc i )  were obtained as follows:
102:where D sc i represents  the  normalized  collective  abnormalities/differences of the structural neighbors of region i, Di is regional abnormalities (unthresholded t statistic) of region i, Ni is the number of neighbors of region i with a structural covariance connection, SC i , j is the strength of structural covariance between region i and region j. For each subtype, the D sc i was predicted using regional abnormalities of the neighboring regions. The Pearson ' s correlation coefficient between true abnormalities and D sc i across all brain regions was calculated.
114:Subsequently, to better understand the relative importance of each predictor (in this case, neurotransmitter receptors/transporters profiles) in contributing to the model ' s overall predictive power, we conducted a dominance  analysis  for  each  multilinear  model.  Dominance  analysis helps determine how each predictor impacts the model ' s fit by considering all possible predictor combinations within the model (Budescu and David, 1993). This is achieved by applying the multilinear model to every potential subset of predictors, resulting in 2 n -1 subset models for a model with n predictors. In our study, we utilized the concept of total dominance to gauge each predictor ' s relative significance (Budescu and David, 1993; Hansen and Shafiei, 2022a). Total dominance is calculated as  the  mean  increase  in  explained  variance  (R 2 )  that  each  predictor contributes across all subset models in which it is included (Azen and Budescu, 2003; Budescu and David, 1993).
138:## 3.4. Transdiagnostic subtype exhibit divergent structural covariance network-informed disease epicenters
162:Next,  we  delved  into  exploring  the  connections  between  neurotransmitter receptors/transporters and gray matter morphological abnormalities  within  transdiagnostic  subtypes.  This  was  achieved  by constructing two multilinear models that accounted for the spatial distributions of receptors/transporters and the differential patterns of gray matter morphological abnormalities in each subtype. The goodness-offit  of  these  models  (adjusted  R 2 )  was  0.16  (F-statistic  (246,226) = 3.41) for subtype 1 and 0.59 (F-statistic (246,226) = 19.70) for subtype 2, indicating strong model performance (see Fig. 5A). Notably, all FDRcorrected permutation p -values were &lt; 0.001. Furthermore, our analysis utilizing  dominance analysis revealed key neurotransmitter receptors associated with each subtype. Specifically, 5HT1A emerged as significant for subtype 1, whereas mGluR5 was identified as crucial for subtype 2 (see Fig. 5B).
172:Using subject-level gray matter volume deviations as features, we successfully identified two robust transdiagnostic subtypes. These subtypes  demonstrated  resilience  across  different  subtyping  algorithms, were replicated  in  independent  datasets,  and  extended  to  previously unseen psychiatric disorders, thereby affirming their reproducibility and generalizability. Notably, they exhibited opposite differential patterns relative  to  healthy  controls  (HCs)  and  distinct  structural  covariance network-informed disease epicenters. Moreover, we observed that gray matter morphological abnormalities in these subtypes were distinctly associated  with  neurotransmitter  receptors/transporters.  Specifically, the abnormal pattern in subtype 1 was predominantly linked to excitatory  receptors,  while  that  in  subtype  2  was  mainly  associated  with inhibitory  receptors,  thereby  providing  insights  into  the  underlying molecular mechanisms of these subtypes. Additionally, we found that the gray matter differential pattern in subtype 2 correlated with transcriptional profiles of inflammation-related genes, whereas such correlation  was  not  evident  in  subtype  1.  This  highlights  the  complex interplay between neurobiological processes and molecular pathways in psychiatric  disorders.  Overall,  our  findings  unveil  two  reproducible transdiagnostic subtypes characterized by distinct gray matter morphological differential patterns and underlying transcriptional and molecular bases. These insights offer valuable contributions to psychiatric nosology, furthering our understanding of the heterogeneous nature of psychiatric disorders.
178:Fig. 4. Association between gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identified subtypes and normal structural covariance network (SC). (A). Pearson ' s correlation coefficients between regional differential pattern and the normalized collective abnormalities/differences of structural neighbors (SC-informed differential pattern) of the identified subtypes. (B). Distributions of putative disease epicenters are illustrated for each subtype.
184:Another significant finding of our study is the observation of divergent disease epicenters within the identified subtypes, as informed by the structural-covariance network. Previous neuroimaging research has highlighted that structural abnormalities in psychiatric disorders tend to adhere to the underlying normal connectome architecture rather than being randomly distributed (Shafiei et al., 2020b; Wen et al., 2024). Typically, structural abnormalities originate from specific disease epicenters and then propagate to other regions of the brain (Shafiei et al., 2020b; Wannan et al., 2019; Zhou et al., 2012). Consistent with these findings, our study revealed that structural abnormalities were significantly inferred from topologically connected neighbors, likely reflecting shared  developmental  or  maturation  trajectories  (Alexander-Bloch et al., 2013b; Han et al., 2023c). Furthermore, we found that the identified subtypes exhibited divergent disease epicenters in regions such as the hippocampus, anterior cingulate cortex, striatum, and insula, which are consistently implicated across various psychiatric disorders
188:Fig. 5. Association between gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identified subtypes and distribution of neurotransmitter receptors/transporters. (A). Multilinear models were constructed to analyze the association between neurotransmitter receptors/transporters and gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identified subtypes. The bar plot displays the corresponding model goodness-of-fit (adjusted R 2 ), with all FDR-corrected permutation p -values being &lt; 0.001. (B). Dominance analysis was employed to determine the relative importance of predictors for each multilinear model. Total dominance values, indicative of the predictors ' relative importance, are depicted. (C). Receptors were categorized into excitatory and inhibitory types, and their accumulated contributions to gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identified subtypes were computed by summing the total dominance values. The accumulated contribution of excitatory receptors (Excitatory) is plotted against that of inhibitory receptors (Inhibitory), with the gray line representing the identity line.
196:(Goodkind et al., 2015). Additional investigations have suggested that these  regions  display  structural  abnormalities  early  in  the  course  of illness  and  may  exert  causal  effects  on  the  structural  abnormalities observed in other brain regions (Han et al., 2023d). Taken together, our results shed light on the association between structural abnormalities observed in the subtypes and the normal structural covariance network. The divergence in disease epicenters among these subtypes likely reflects distinct trajectories of pathological progression.
206:In  conclusion,  we have identified two robust transdiagnostic subtypes based on individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities. These subtypes exhibit contrasting patterns of gray matter morphological  abnormalities  relative  to  healthy  controls  and  have  divergent structural  covariance  network-informed  disease  epicenters.  Furthermore, the gray matter morphological abnormalities in these subtypes are distinctly associated with neurotransmitter receptors/transporters and transcriptional profiles of inflammation-related genes, indicating their differential  molecular  and  genetic  underpinnings.  Our  findings  offer novel insights into psychiatric nosology and underscore the importance of considering transdiagnostic approaches in psychiatric research.
