Fang2024.pdf.md:30:In  contemporary diagnostic  practices,  individuals  presenting  with psychiatric symptom profiles are typically assigned distinct clinical diagnoses. However, accumulating multifactorial evidence suggests that psychiatric  disorders,  including  schizophrenia,  bipolar  disorder  (BD), depression, and attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), share a common neuropathology (Anon, 2013; Anttila et al., 2018; Barch and Sheffield,  2014;  Patel  et  al.,  2021).  For  instance,  over  50  %  of  individuals meeting criteria for one psychiatric disorder also meet criteria for another (Grisanzio et al., 2018). Moreover, many medications and psychotherapies  have  demonstrated  efficacy  in  reducing  symptoms
Fang2024.pdf.md:80:The CNP dataset, approved by the Institutional Review Boards at UCLA and  the  Los  Angeles  County  Department  of  Mental  Health,  includes multimodal  imaging  from  142  patients  with  psychiatric  disorders, including schizophrenia ( n = 50), bipolar disorder (BD, n = 49), and attention  deficit  hyperactivity  disorder  (ADHD, n = 43),  as  well  as healthy controls (HCs, n = 130). Diagnoses were based on the Structured Clinical Interview for DSM-IV-Text Revision (Poldrack et al., 2016). The validation  dataset  1  was  obtained  from  the  Centre  for  Biomedical Research  Excellence  (COBRE)  and  is  available  at  http://fcon\_1000. projects.nitrc.org/indi/retro/cobre.html (Collin et al., 2014). The COBRE group comprised 72 patients with schizophrenia and matched 74 HCs. The validation dataset 2 was obtained from site 20 of the Depression Imaging Research Consortium (DIRECT) (Chen et al., 2022; Yan et al., 2019). The single-site dataset with the largest sample size (site 20) was  used  to  prevent  multi-site  effects  on  the  results.  This  dataset comprised data from 282 patients with depression and 251 matched HCs. All study procedures were in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki of 1975 and approved by the local Research Ethics Committees. More  details  regarding  the  datasets,  data  acquisition,  and  data  preprocessing are described in the Supplementary Methods.
Fang2024.pdf.md:124:## 3.1. Clinical demographics
Fang2024.pdf.md:126:The clinical and demographic information of datasets used in this study was included in Table S1.
Fang2024.pdf.md:174:Psychiatric disorders exhibit significant heterogeneity, manifesting in  diverse  symptoms,  disease  trajectories,  treatment  responses,  and neuroimaging profiles among patients (Bondar et al., 2020; Drysdale et  al.,  2017;  Krishnan  and  Nestler,  2008;  Nguyen  et  al.,  2022).  This heterogeneity poses a substantial challenge and often leads to conflicting findings in research (Chen et al., 2016). For instance, Chand et al. identified  two  subtypes  of  schizophrenia:  subtype  1  displayed  widespread decreased gray matter volume across the brain, whereas subtype 2 exhibited increased gray matter volume in the basal ganglia and internal capsule (Chand et al., 2020). Similar subtypes have been reported in  other  psychiatric disorders  as  well  (Han et  al.,  2022b;  Han  et  al., 2023e). In our study, we addressed this heterogeneity by identifying two replicated transdiagnostic subtypes with opposing abnormal gray matter morphological patterns. This reconciliation helps to clarify contradictory findings from previous neuroimaging studies (Bondar et al., 2020; Drysdale et al., 2017; Krishnan and Nestler, 2008; L ´ azaro et al., 2011; Okasha et al., 2000). Although structural abnormalities in subtype 1 were  not  significant  in  the  discovery  dataset,  likely  due  to  its  small sample size, the validation datasets consistently replicated the opposite differential patterns. Furthermore, sensitivity analyses underscored the
Fang2024.pdf.md:202:investigated  their  associations  with  neurotransmitter  receptor/transporter  profiles  and  transcriptional  profiles  of  inflammation-related genes.  Neurotransmitter  dysfunction  is  widely  recognized  as  a  key aspect  of  the  pathology  of  psychiatric  disorders,  and  the  efficacy  of modern  antidepressants  and  antipsychotics  hinges  on  the  selective modulation of neurotransmitter function (Hansen and Shafiei, 2022a). Indeed, neurotransmitter receptor profiles have been implicated in both structural and functional dysfunctions observed in patients with psychiatric disorders (Hansen and Shafiei, 2022a, 2022b). Consistent with these findings, our study revealed significant associations between the distribution of neurotransmitter receptors/transporters and the structural  abnormalities  observed  in  the  identified  subtypes.  Moreover, dominance analysis results unveiled distinct contributions of excitatory and  inhibitory  receptors  to  the  structural  abnormalities  observed  in these subtypes. Furthermore, our findings indicated that the structural abnormalities  of  subtype  2  were  linked  to  transcriptional  profiles  of inflammation-related genes. This finding aligns with the inflammation hypothesis of psychosis (Thylur and Goldsmith, 2022). Together, these results provide detailed insights into the distinct molecular and genetic bases underlying the identified subtypes.
Fang2024.pdf.md:204:This study is not without its limitations. Firstly, the number of psychiatric disorders included in our analysis is limited. However, despite being identified based on three primary psychiatric disorders, the two transdiagnostic subtypes demonstrated generalizability to unseen psychiatric  disorders.  Future  investigations  could  explore  whether  these subtypes hold validity across a broader spectrum of psychiatric conditions. Secondly, due to a lack of sufficient clinical information such as education level, alcohol and especially for medication dosage, we were unable to examine their effects on the clustering outcomes. This represents an important avenue for future research to elucidate the influence of medication on the identified subtypes. Thirdly, we did not account for comorbidities in our analysis. Future studies should investigate whether comorbidities impact our results and whether they contribute to further subtyping within psychiatric disorders.
Fang2024.pdf.md:248:- Bondar, J., Caye, A., Chekroud, A.M., Kieling, C., 2020. Symptom clusters in adolescent depression and differential response to treatment: a secondary analysis of the Treatment for Adolescents with Depression Study randomised trial. Lancet Psychiatry 7, 337 -343.
Fang2024.pdf.md:272:- Grisanzio, K.A., Goldstein-Piekarski, A.N., Wang, M.Y., Ahmed, A.P.R., Samara, Z., Williams, L.M., 2018. Transdiagnostic symptom clusters and associations with brain, behavior, and daily function in mood, anxiety, and trauma disorders. JAMA Psychiatry 75, 201 -209.
Fang2024.pdf.md:303:- Marquand, A.F., Rezek, I., Buitelaar, J., Beckmann, C.F., 2016. Understanding heterogeneity in clinical cohorts using normative models: beyond case-control studies. Biol. Psychiatry 80, 552 -561.
Fang2024.pdf.md:319:- Radhakrishnan, R., Matuskey, D., Nabulsi, N., Gaiser, E., Gallezot, J.D., Henry, S., Planeta, B., Lin, S.F., Ropchan, J., Huang, Y., Carson, R.E., D ' Souza, D.C., 2020. In vivo 5-HT(6) and 5-HT(2A) receptor availability in antipsychotic treated schizophrenia patients vs. unmedicated healthy humans measured with [(11)C] GSK215083 PET. Psychiatry research. Neuroimaging 295, 111007.
